  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "RkmH6Yl8nzGkbTNZBEuyn3d4mbU=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:51:07.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "An industry vendor's framing of the post-BIM gap (BIM Services LLC, Mar 2026) \u2014 independently corroborating the practitioner diagnosis from DT BAU:\n\nBIM has been instrumental in creating structured digital environments, but it primarily addresses **geometric and coordination challenges** (visualization, clash detection, design handoff). Documentation workflows stay largely manual:\n\n- Professionals manage massive technical documents, often **1000+ pages**: submittals, transmittals, RFIs (Requests for Information), compliance documents, project approvals\n- Heavy reliance on manual data entry and interpretation\n- Limited integration between models and textual/compliance-based data\n- Fragmented workflows between BIM models and supporting documents\n- Repetitive, error-prone administrative tasks divert skilled professionals from decision-making, on-site problem-solving, and coordination\n\nThe stated conclusion: to \"work smarter,\" the industry must extend BIM into intelligent, **document-aware ecosystems** \u2014 the same layer above 3D models that Powerhouse's construction positioning targets. Notably, the article dismisses the usual sensors/drones framing of construction AI in favor of \"making sense of messy, unstructured data like specs and contracts\" \u2014 cognitive automation over field automation.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T18:51:07.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T18:51:07.000Z"
    }
  },
